{{Short description|Aspect of Mexican-American literature}}
[[File:Day one- Diverse Cultures Innovator Luis Rodriguez.jpg|thumb|222x222px|[[Luis J. Rodriguez]] (2009)]]{{Chicano and Mexican American topics sidebar}}
'''Chicano literature''' is an aspect of [[Mexican-American literature]] that emerged from the [[Chicanismo|cultural consciousness]] developed in the [[Chicano Movement]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Castillo |first=Rafael C. |date=2019 |title=Chicano Literature |url=https://www.oxfordbibliographies.com/display/document/obo-9780199913701/obo-9780199913701-0140.xml |access-date=2023-01-25 |website=Oxford Bibliographies |language=en |quote=Chicano literature is therefore written by a group of people who identify with the political, cultural, and social Chicano movement... to document the history of Chicano consciousness in the United States... Chicano literature then becomes a response and counter-narrative to the hegemony of American literature that excluded ethnic voices not germane to the Anglo-American literary heritage.}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Introduction to Chicano Literature |url=https://anaya.unm.edu/chicanoliterature |access-date=2023-01-25 |website=anaya.unm.edu |quote=Although “Chicano” and “Mexican-American” are often used interchangeably, the former indicates an added political sensibility; an asserted self-awareness of a cultural identity that cannot be separated from social and material struggles for equality and inclusion.}}</ref> Chicano literature formed out of the political and cultural struggle of [[Chicano|Chicana/os]] to develop a political foundation and [[Cultural identity|identity]] that rejected [[Anglo-Americans|Anglo-American]] hegemony.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Trejo |first=Arnulfo D. |date=1979 |title=As We See Ourselves in Chicano Literature |url=https://open.uapress.arizona.edu/read/194e052d-0fe6-402a-9149-a081e98ec011/section/e9247245-9a0f-4154-a16c-f7ba863e2a85 |access-date=2023-01-26 |website=University of Arizona Press |language=en-US}}</ref> This literature embraced the [[Pre-Columbian era|pre-Columbian]] roots of Mexican-Americans, especially those who identify as Chicana/os.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":4" />

Chicano literature first emerged in the mid-1960s and is notable for its early embrace of [[Spanglish]] in published literature as well as its use of hybrid forms and styles.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=TSHA {{!}} Chicano Literary Renaissance |url=https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/chicano-literary-renaissance |access-date=2023-01-26 |website=www.tshaonline.org}}</ref> Chicana/o writers often include earlier published literature as residing within or as being a precursor to the tradition.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Daily Chela |date=2023-01-10 |title=A 400 Year Visual History Of Chicano Literature |url=https://www.dailychela.com/chicano-literature-visual-history/ |access-date=2023-01-25 |website=The Daily Chela |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=1981-11-22 |title=Chicano Literature |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1981/11/22/books/l-chicano-literature-101917.html |access-date=2023-01-26 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In addition to [[prose]], [[Chicano poetry]] and [[playwriting]] are included as forms of Chicano literature.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

{{Short description|Organoberyllium Complex in Main Group Chemistry}}

[[File:Crystal Structure of a BePh2 Complex that produce dimers.png|thumb|229px|Structure of diphenylberyllium, which has the formula {{chem2|Be3(C6H5)6}}.<ref name=Ph2Be/>]]
'''Organoberyllium chemistry''' involves the synthesis and properties of [[organometallic compound]]s featuring the group 2 [[alkaline earth]] metal [[beryllium]] (Be).<ref name=Coates/> The area remains less developed relative to the chemistry of other [[main-group element]]s, because Be compounds are toxic and few applications have been found.<ref>{{Citation |last=Gad |first=S. C. |title=Beryllium |date=2014-01-01 |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123864543008204 |encyclopedia=Encyclopedia of Toxicology (Third Edition) |pages=435–437 |editor-last=Wexler |editor-first=Philip |access-date=2022-10-27 |place=Oxford |publisher=Academic Press |language=en |isbn=978-0-12-386455-0}}</ref>

