{{Short description|Process that separates layered materials into nanomaterials}}
{{Other uses|Exfoliation (disambiguation)}}

In [[chemistry]], '''exfoliation''' is a process that separates [[layered materials]] into [[nanomaterials]] by breaking the bonds between layers using mechanical, chemical, or thermal procedures.

While exfoliation has historical roots dating back centuries, significant advances and widespread research gained momentum after [[Konstantin Novoselov|Novoselov]] and [[Andre Geim|Geim]]'s [[discovery of graphene]] using [[Scotch tape]] in 2004. Their Nobel Prize-winning research primarily relied on mechanical exfoliation for the production of [[graphene]] which sparked an immediate interest in the exfoliation process. Today, exfoliation is regarded as the most widely used nanomaterial production technique.

Exfoliation typically involves breaking weak bonds called [[Van Der Waals' Bond|van der Waals bonds]] to create two-dimensional materials, such as [[graphene]] or [[transition metal dichalcogenide monolayers]]. While various reversible chemical processes, such as [[Intercalation (chemistry)|intercalation]] can disrupt the weak bonds in a [[lamellar structure]] and introduce guest species, many of them fail to produce single-sheet materials as the processes are not strong enough to cancel the interlayer attractions.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Whittingha |first=Stanley M. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qrFVZwdVrVgC&dq=intercalation&pg=PP1 |title=Intercalation Chemistry |date=2012-12-02 |publisher=Elsevier |isbn=978-0-323-14040-9 |language=en}}</ref> However, during exfoliation, the high energy input leads to an extreme bond-breaking process that irreversibly separates the layers into single sheets. Lately,{{When|date=May 2026}} it has been shown that if the energy input is substantial enough, the procedure can even break much stronger, bonds such as [[Metallic bonding|metallic]] or [[Ionic bonding|ionic]] bonds to create non-van der Waals materials like hematene or other nanoplatelets.<ref name=":11">{{Cite journal |last1=Puthirath Balan |first1=Aravind |last2=Radhakrishnan |first2=Sruthi |last3=Woellner |first3=Cristiano F. |last4=Sinha |first4=Shyam K. |last5=Deng |first5=Liangzi |last6=Reyes |first6=Carlos de los |last7=Rao |first7=Banki Manmadha |last8=Paulose |first8=Maggie |last9=Neupane |first9=Ram |last10=Apte |first10=Amey |last11=Kochat |first11=Vidya |last12=Vajtai |first12=Robert |last13=Harutyunyan |first13=Avetik R. |last14=Chu |first14=Ching-Wu |last15=Costin |first15=Gelu |date=July 2018 |title=Exfoliation of a non-van der Waals material from iron ore hematite |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41565-018-0134-y |journal=Nature Nanotechnology |language=en |volume=13 |issue=7 |pages=602–609 |doi=10.1038/s41565-018-0134-y |pmid=29736036 |bibcode=2018NatNa..13..602P |issn=1748-3395|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Exfoliation has practical applications in fields such as electronics, biomedicine, and [[aerospace engineering]]. It can be used to create materials with properties tailored for specific uses such as efficient energy storage or lightweight and durable materials.

